# Customer release

## Rollout order

Use the normal one-instance-at-a-time rollout. With no hard ordering constraint in
`plan.json`, the estate's default alphabetical order deploys `customer` before
`notifier`. Keep that order so the explicit-consent rule is in place before the
notifier daily-cap change is fully rolled out. Queued events do not need to drain
before deployment.

## Mixed-version behavior

Old and new customer instances use the same database schema and the same
`offer.queued {customerId, offerId}` event contract. During a mixed customer
rollout, an old instance may still queue an offer for an `implied` customer, while
new instances do not; the new notifier can consume either event shape. Once all
customer instances are new, implied consent produces neither `allowed=true` nor
an offer event. Existing queued events remain valid and are processed normally.

## Rollback

If the customer rollout fails, stop it and route traffic back to the previous
customer version. No schema or event migration is needed. Rollback does not
remove already queued or sent offers; those durable records remain valid. If the
notifier has already been upgraded, it continues consuming the unchanged event
format, including events emitted by the rolled-back customer.
